--TEST--
A cycle survives the graph copy as a cycle rather than unrolling or hanging, and the sharing inside it stays sharing rather than becoming two nodes — `rule:classes/graph-copy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/graph-copy`'s two hardest sentences, as a program: "a cycle (`$a->self =
// $a`) terminates instead of recursing forever", and "shared substructure
// inside the source stays shared inside the copy". Terminating is only half of
// the first one — a walk that ran off the end of an unrolled chain would also
// terminate — so every assertion below is made by walking the copy rather than
// by observing that the encode returned.
//
// `next` and `alias` are non-nullable and self-referential from the
// constructor, so every node here is already on a cycle before a copy is asked
// for and no case below needs a null to stand in for "the end".
final class Node {
    public string $name;
    public Node $next;
    public Node $alias;

    public function constructor(string $name) {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->next = $this;
        $this->alias = $this;
    }
}

// Two nodes pointing at each other, so a walk of an odd length lands on the
// other name: a copy that had collapsed the pair into one object lands on the
// first, and one that unrolled the cycle cannot be walked 101 times at all.
var $a = new Node("a");
var $b = new Node("b");
$a->next = $b;
$b->next = $a;

var $copy = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($a)) as Node;

var $walk = $copy;
int $steps = 0;
while ($steps < 101) {
    $walk = $walk->next;
    $steps += 1;
}
echo $steps, " steps, at ", $walk->name, "\n";

// The self-cycle the ADR spells out by name, which is the one-node case the
// two-node walk cannot distinguish from a bug.
var $self = new Node("self");
var $selfCopy = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($self)) as Node;
echo $selfCopy->next->next->next->name, "\n";

// The copy shares no mutable state with its source: writing through the
// copy's cycle moves the copy's own node and nothing on this side of it.
$copy->next->next->name = "rewritten";
echo $copy->name, " ", $a->name, "\n";

// Sharing inside a cycle. Three nodes round a ring, and `alias` is a second
// reference to the node `next` already reaches — so the walk arrives at one
// node by two routes. A copy that duplicated the shared node instead of
// sharing it prints the original name on the second route while still walking
// the ring correctly, which is why this is asserted by writing and reading
// back rather than by counting hops.
var $one = new Node("one");
var $two = new Node("two");
var $three = new Node("three");
$one->next = $two;
$two->next = $three;
$three->next = $one;
$one->alias = $two;

var $ring = Core\Serialize::decode(Core\Serialize::encode($one)) as Node;
$ring->alias->name = "shared";
echo $ring->next->name, " ", $ring->next->next->name, "\n";
--EXPECT--
101 steps, at b
self
rewritten a
shared three
